{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>At the end of seven years you shall make a release.</b> It seems correct to me that this admonishes concerning the seventh year itself, that we make it a release in plowing and harvest, as it says (Exodus 23:11), <b>but in the seventh you shall let it rest and lie fallow</b>. And this is the meaning of <b>you shall make a release</b> — that you cease, in the manner of <b>making the Sabbath day</b> (Deuteronomy 5:15). And it abbreviated the prohibition of sowing and pruning, for it already mentioned them in the exposition (Leviticus 25:4), but it added to explain that it is a release to YHWH also in the remission of monies. And this is the meaning of <b>for he called it a release to YHWH</b>, that it is a Sabbath to YHWH and all labors shall cease, and I have already hinted at its secret (ibid. v. 2).\n\nAs for the meaning of <b>at the end</b>, R. Abraham ibn Ezra (on Deut. 15:1) said: at the beginning of the year. And so said all the grammarians, that the beginning and the end are both called קֵץ (end/extremity), for every thing has two extremities, as in the verses <b>from one end to the other end</b> (Exodus 26:28), <b>at its two ends</b> (Exodus 25:19), <b>on its four corners</b> (Exodus 27:4). But their words are not correct in my view, for the beginning of seven years is the first year, and that is the extremity called in their view \"beginning\"; and if Scripture had said <b>at the end of the seventh year</b>, their interpretation would be correct.\n\nAccording to our Sages (Sifrei 122), <b>at the end</b> means at the end of the seven, and Scripture speaks only of the remission of monies: it says, at the end of every count of seven years you shall make a release, so that every creditor shall remit what he holds; and therefore they said (Arakhin 28a) that the seventh year remits only at its end.\n\nAnd likewise according to them (ibid.), <b>at the end of seven years, at the appointed time of the year of release, on the festival of Sukkot</b> — at the end of the seven, except that the קֵץ mentioned here is immediate and the קֵץ mentioned there is removed, for it specifies <b>at the appointed time of the year of release, on the festival of Sukkot</b>. Now the meaning of both is <b>from the conclusion</b>: here it says, at the conclusion of seven years you shall make a release, and there it says likewise, when the seven years conclude, at the appointed time of the year of release, you shall hold the assembly.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense, it appears to me that the language of Scripture as plainly understood is clear and correct, for קֵץ means end, and so is its Aramaic translation, and numbers and all things have a beginning and an end, and their beginning and end are within them — as you would say regarding the tens, that the first is the beginning of the number and the tenth is its end. If so, <b>קֵץ שֶׁבַע שָׁנִים</b> (the end of seven years) is the seventh year, which is the end of that number, and Scripture speaks of the release of the land, as I explained above.\n\nAnd the meaning of <b>מִקֵּץ</b> is like <b>one cherub at this end</b> ... <b>and one cherub at that end</b> (Exodus 25:19), and it is as though it said: in the seventh year you shall make it a release, that you cease in it.\n\nAnd likewise, <b>At the end of seven years you shall let go every one his brother, a Hebrew, who has been sold to you and has served you six years</b> (Jeremiah 34:14) — its meaning is in the seventh year, which is the end of the number, and it resembles what is written in the Torah (Exodus 21:2), <b>and in the seventh he shall go out free</b>, and likewise <b>and in the seventh year you shall let him go free from yourself</b> (Deut. 15:12). For since it said <b>and has served you six years</b>, it was unnecessary to specify anything other than that in the seventh year he goes free.\n\nNow according to this approach it would appear that the meaning of the other verse, <b>at the end of seven years, at the appointed time of the year of release, on the festival of Sukkot</b>, would be similarly at the time of the seventh year — on the festival of Sukkot at the beginning of that year in which plowing and sowing are forbidden, which is the year of release. But we cannot say this, for our Sages (Sotah 41a) received and saw that the assembly took place on the festival of Sukkot of the eighth year, which is the year after the release. Therefore the commentators said that the meaning of <b>at the appointed time of the year of release</b> is: at the appointed time of the first year of the count toward the release. But this is not correct, for Scripture never calls <b>שְׁנַת הַשְּׁמִטָּה</b> (the year of release) anything other than the year of rest itself, <b>the year that is a release to YHWH</b>, and likewise <b>שְׁנַת הַיּוֹבֵל</b> (the year of Jubilee).\n\nOur Sages themselves were precise about this, for they said (Arakhin 24b): What verse speaks of <b>in the year of Jubilee</b>? It is <b>from the year of Jubilee</b> — the year after the Jubilee.\n\nBut this is what I say: קֵץ and סוֹף (end) are said in Scripture regarding the after-end of every thing, and sometimes they are within it and sometimes after it, outside of it: <b>from the edge of his brothers</b> (Genesis 47:2), <b>at the edge of the joining</b> (Exodus 26:4), <b>some of them shall stand before the king</b> (Daniel 1:5), <b>from the tip of its wings to the tip of its wings</b> (I Kings 6:24) — all of these are the extremity of a thing joined to it, and it is within it. <b>The end of all flesh has come before Me</b> (Genesis 6:13), <b>he shall come to his end and there is none to help him</b> (Daniel 11:45), <b>the end has come</b> (Ezekiel 7:2), <b>an end has come</b> (ibid. v. 6) — all of these are the termination of the thing mentioned, and it is after it, cut off and severed.\n\nAnd likewise the concept with the term סוֹף: <b>It is better to go to the house of mourning</b> ... <b>for that is the end of all man</b> (Ecclesiastes 7:2) — after him, for he has already died; <b>they are utterly consumed with terrors</b> (Psalms 73:19) [using a term of end]; <b>the deed that Elohim has done from beginning to end</b> (Ecclesiastes 3:11) — in the deed itself. And in their language (Bava Batra 13a): <b>כְּדֵי עַמּוּד</b> (approximately a column's width) at its beginning, and <b>כְּדֵי הֶקֵּף</b> at its end.\n\nAnd the meaning of the verse will be: at the conclusion of seven years, in the reckoning of the year of release, on the festival of Sukkot that comes after the seven, you shall hold the assembly — and so that it not appear to them that these are seven years for a different count, it specified that the seven shall be during the time of release.\n\nAnd likewise <b>כִּי אֶקַּח מוֹעֵד</b> (Psalms 75:3) means an appointed time, and likewise all the מוֹעֲדִים are appointed times. And this קֵץ is after the number, as it specifies <b>on the festival of Sukkot</b>, while <b>at the end of seven years you shall make a release</b> includes the number itself. And there was no need to elaborate on this, for it already explained that the seventh year is the year of release, and קֵץ will have a broad application throughout the release of the entire year, as I explained according to the plain sense.\n\nAnd it is possible to say that Scripture counts the beginning of the year from the festival of Sukkot, as it says (Exodus 34:22), <b>and the festival of ingathering, the turn of the year</b>, and it says (Exodus 23:16), <b>and the festival of ingathering at the going out of the year</b> — if so, <b>at the end of seven years</b> would be at the end and outside of it, immediately adjacent. And it says <b>at the appointed time of the year of release</b> so that the count of seven is by releases, as I explained — not that the release extends until this festival.\n\nAnd I saw one of the authors who wrote, seeking to prove that one adds from the profane onto the holy, and that the release was in effect until the festival of Sukkot of the eighth year. But this matter is refuted in the Talmud (Rosh Hashanah 12a) by clear proofs, so it is not true.\n\nAnd it is possible to say that what our Sages said regarding <b>at the end of seven years you shall make a release</b> — that the remission of monies takes effect only at its end — is a midrash, because it did not say <b>in the seventh year you shall make a release</b>, hinting that at the end of the seven you make a release more than at the beginning, and their intent in this קֵץ is outside of it. And a person may collect his debt on the last day of the year of release and it is not remitted until nightfall, for so we find in the Tosefta (Shevi'it 8:11), that they write a prosbol on the eve of Rosh Hashanah of the year following the seventh year."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Where Ramban quotes brief Hebrew phrases from rabbinic sources (e.g., כְּדֵי עַמּוּד / כְּדֵי הֶקֵּף from Bava Batra 13a), these are left in Hebrew as Ramban cites them, since his argument turns on their precise wording."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:50:56.503418366Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}